152
00:21:35,000 --> 00:21:41,000
 I would have to assume some form of underlying distributed.

153
00:21:41,000 --> 00:21:54,000
 Now since heights are real value, I could model this as a Gaussian, but you see this has a problem because a Gaussian can generate negative real numbers as well.

154
00:21:54,000 --> 00:21:56,000
 And we know that heights cannot be negative.

155
00:21:56,000 --> 00:22:07,000
 So Gaussian is not really the ideal distribution, but you know, we can model it as a Gaussian such that it has very low probability of being being negative.

156
00:22:07,000 --> 00:22:18,000
 So we will go with a Gaussian and then we will say that the heights are drawn from a Gaussian distribution with some mean mu and standard deviation sigma.

157
00:22:18,000 --> 00:22:23,000
 And then, of course, we don't know what the mean and the standard deviation are.

158
00:22:23,000 --> 00:22:41,000
 So if we are given some examples of heights of actual people in the population, then we can estimate the values of mu, which I note as mu hat and the value of sigma, which I denote as sigma hat from these samples.

159
00:22:41,000 --> 00:22:48,000
 And now when I have mu hat and sigma hat, now I can draw samples from the Gaussian, right?

160
00:22:48,000 --> 00:23:01,000
 And these values, when I keep on drawing them, will actually look like values coming from or values of, which are denote heights of people from that population.

161
00:23:01,000 --> 00:23:15,000
 So if the data that was given to me was for, you know, a very tall race like some people from Denmark or Netherlands or like that, then the samples that I will draw from here are probably going to be six feet, etc.

162
00:23:15,000 --> 00:23:24,000
