 So when you draw samples from the Bernoulli distribution, it gives you a head or a tail, right?

175
00:24:33,000 --> 00:24:38,000
 And the parameter is rho, so which is really the probability of head, right?

176
00:24:38,000 --> 00:24:54,000
 Now, the way to sample values and sort of see whether a person is going to be there at the counter on the particular second or not, is to draw a value from this Bernoulli distribution and see whether heads comes out or tails comes out.

177
00:24:54,000 --> 00:25:07,000
 Now, if I, somebody gives me training data, right, about whether a person came to this counter in some second or not, right, then I could estimate the probability of head from the value that was given to me.

178
00:25:07,000 --> 00:25:26,000
 And then, once I have estimated this probability of head, now I can sample values from this Bernoulli distribution with the probability of head rho hat, right, and simulate the situation about whether somebody is going to come to a counter or not, right?

179
00:25:26,000 --> 00:25:41,000
 So, for example, if you, if the sample data gave you a scenario where people come to the counter very infrequently, then the probability of head that you will probably estimate from that is going to be close, closer to 0 than to 1.

180
00:25:41,000 --> 00:25:51,000
 On the other hand, if there is somebody who is always there at the counter during a busy period, the probability of head that you will estimate is probably going to be very close to 1, right?

181
00:25:51,000 --> 00:26:01,000
 So, that is how we can learn to simulate the arrival of people at a counter, for example, okay.

182
00:26:01,000 --> 00:26:11,000
 Now, I have been talking about sampling from a distribution, right, but how do you actually sample from a distribution?

183
00:26:11,000 --> 00:26:21,000
 Okay, so I would want to want you to think about this. Of course, there are things that you can read up, right?

184
00:26:21,000 --> 00:26:47,000
 For example, there is this famous method called the Box Muller method for how to draw samples from a Gaussian, right? But if I ask you that, you know, I am giving you a Bernoulli distribution whose probability of head is 0.75 and I want you to simulate draws from this distribution, okay?

185
00:26:47,000 --> 00:26:54,000
 How would you go about doing that? Okay, I would want you to think about this problem.

186
00:26:54,000 --> 00:27:07,000
 The answer in short is you have to form something called the cumulative density function and sample using that, but these are not things to memorize, these are things to understand, right?

187
00:27:07,000 --> 00:27:16,000
 So, I am sure you will be able to figure it out if you think about yourself or sometime, okay?

188
00:27:16,000 --> 00:27:26,000
 Now, here is another, my next googly question, right? So, this is to impress on you the power of sampling, okay?

189
00:27:26,000 --> 00:27:35,000
 So, what is sampling here? I am going to throw dots onto a wall. So, where it is going to land on the wall is of course random.

190
00:27:35,000 --> 00:27:44,000
 Why is that random? Because, you know, I do not have sufficient control so that whenever I throw a dot in a wall, I can land it exactly where I want.

191
00:27:44,000 --> 00:27:51,000
 I do not have that kind of control. So, I can think of it as a random variable where it lands on the wall, right?

192
00:27:51,000 --> 00:28:01,000
 So, if somebody gave you a dot, right? And said you are allowed to throw dots and, okay, if some damage is done to my wall, that is fine.

193
00:28:01,000 --> 00:28:08,000
 Can you estimate the value of pi just by doing that, okay?

194
00:28:08,000 --> 00:28:20,000
 And the answer is yes. And I will leave you to find out how you get to that, but this is just so that you understand the power of drawing samples, okay?

195
00:28:20,000 --> 00:28:31,000
 You can actually estimate the value of pi to as much accuracy as you want just by throwing dots onto a wall, all right?

196
00:28:31,000 --> 00:28:38,000
 Okay, so now let us come to the formal definition of what is a generative model.

197
00:28:38,000 --> 00:28:46,000
 So, one simple way to think of a generative model is that it is a program. It is nothing but a program, the way you all write programs.

198
00:28:46,000 --> 00:29:04,000
 But it is a program that has a probability distribution somewhere without inside it, such that every time you run the program, even with the same quote, you get a different output, right?

199
00:29:04,000 --> 00:29:14,000
 So, the output is random, okay? And it is not arbitrarily random. The output follows a particular distribution.

200
00:29:14,000 --> 00:29:22,000
 So, then that kind of a program or a probabilistic program is one way to define a generative model.

201
00:29:22,000 --> 00:29:29,000
 So, it is a programming language equipped with a sampling operator, right? And this is often called a simulation.

202
00:29:29,000 --> 00:29:34,000
 So, you hear about people simulating the weather, simulating the traffic and things like that.

203
00:29:34,000 --> 00:29:42,000
 So, all of these are done using probabilistic programs, okay? So, these are examples of generative models, okay?

204
00:29:42,000 --> 00:29:52,000
 Now, I mentioned IID before. So, what IID means is independently and identically distributed.

205
00:29:52,000 --> 00:30:05,000
 So, identically distributed in this, in this slide means that every xi or all i is actually drawn from exactly the same distribution.

206
00:30:05,000 --> 00:30:14,000
 Let us say the same Gaussian distribution with the same mean and radius, okay? They are identically distributed.

207
00:30:14,000 --> 00:30:30,000
 But what does this other part mean independently? What this means is that every xi is drawn without looking at what other xi's have previously been drawn, okay?

208
00:30:30,000 --> 00:30:37,000
 And what independently? Every draw is independent of all the previous draws.

209
00:30:37,000 --> 00:30:43,000
 And, you know, the example that I showed you will of course give you IID samples.

210
00:30:43,000 --> 00:30:51,000
 But you can imagine that for many real scenarios, the IID assumption clearly does not hold it, right?

211
00:30:51,000 --> 00:30:57,000
 What is an example of this? So, if you are trying to model the temperature of a day.

212
00:30:57,000 --> 00:31:05,000
 The temperature of a day very clearly is dependent on the temperatures of a few previous days, right?

213
00:31:05,000 --> 00:31:15,000
 So, you will have a much better ability to predict today's temperature if you had information about the temperatures of the past previous days, right?

214
00:31:15,000 --> 00:31:27,000
 So, x t plus 1 in this case is clearly not independent of x 1, x 2, sorry, that should have been x 1, x 1, x 2, dot, dot, dot, dot, x t, right?

215
00:31:27,000 --> 00:31:37,000
 And in fact, it may not even follow exactly the same distribution as x t plus x t or x t minus 1 or x 2, right?

216
00:31:37,000 --> 00:31:44,000
 So, these are examples of a situation where an IID model is not going to work, okay?

217
00:31:44,000 --> 00:31:49,000
 So, what are examples of non-IID models, okay?

218
00:31:49,000 --> 00:32:02,000
 So, now here let's say I want to model the temperature of 10 days or n days and that is denoted as x 1, x 2, x 3, x 4 up to x n, right?

219
00:32:02,000 --> 00:32:09,000
 So, I want to model a joint distribution over p x 1, x 2, x 3, x 4 up to x n.

220
00:32:09,000 --> 00:32:28,000
 What does the chain rule of probability tell me that I can always write this as p of x 1 into p of x 2 given x 1, p of x 3 given x 1, x 2, dot, dot, dot, p of x n given x 1, x 2, x 3, x 4 up to x n minus 1.

221
00:32:28,000 --> 00:32:34,000
 There are no assumptions here. This is always true. This is the chain rule of probability.

222
00:32:34,000 --> 00:32:43,000
 But what is the problem with this, right? So, just look at the last term here, right?

223
00:32:43,000 --> 00:32:51,000
 So, p of x n condition on x 1, x 2, x 3, x 4, x n minus 1.

224
00:32:51,000 --> 00:33:02,000
 So, what this means is that for different combinations of values of x 1 to x n minus 1, the distribution is going to be different.

225
00:33:02,000 --> 00:33:09,000
 And how many different combinations of values can you have for x 1 up to x n minus 1?

226
00:33:09,000 --> 00:33:15,000
 Even these, each of these can take one of two values, 0 or 1, right?

227
00:33:15,000 --> 00:33:18,000
 You could have 2 to the power n minus 1 possibilities, right?

228
00:33:18,000 --> 00:33:26,000
 So, you would actually have to learn or store those many distributions, which is exponential in number, right?

229
00:33:26,000 --> 00:33:34,000
 If you have to model this just using the chain rule of probability, okay? So, that is typically not done in practice.

230
00:33:34,000 --> 00:33:40,000
 I will come to how it can be done. So, that is where neural networks are helpful, okay? I will come to that.

231
00:33:40,000 --> 00:33:44,000
 But before neural networks became the fashion, right?

232
00:33:44,000 --> 00:33:51,000
 This was often done by one approximation is called the IID modeling assumption, right?

233
00:33:51,000 --> 00:34:03,000
 Where you say, okay, I simply do not care about the conditioning. I will simply like x of i given x i x 1 to x minus 1 as p of x i for all i.

234
00:34:03,000 --> 00:34:09,000
 I do not care about the conditioning at all. This is an assumption, right?

235
00:34:09,000 --> 00:34:12,000
 And this is the IID modeling assumption.

236
00:34:12,000 --> 00:34:21,000
 So, now you can see the IID modeling assumption as some kind of an approximation of the truth to join this, right?

237
00:34:21,000 --> 00:34:26,000
 Now, there can be compromises somewhere in the middle.

238
00:34:26,000 --> 00:34:31,000
 And one kind of compromise is called a Markov modeling assumption, right?

239
00:34:31,000 --> 00:34:40,000
 So, here I will say that x i, you know, it really does not depend on everything from x 1 to x i minus 1.

240
00:34:40,000 --> 00:34:46,000
 x i only depends on the previously seen value, which is x i minus 1.

241
00:34:46,000 --> 00:34:53,000
 Once I know x i minus 1, I do not really care about all the preceding values, okay?

242
00:34:53,000 --> 00:35:02,000
 Now you see, just compare the x i given x i minus 1 term here and the last term of the previous change.

243
00:35:02,000 --> 00:35:14,000
 Now you should be able to see why this significantly reduces the number of possible distributions that I have to store, the number of parameters that I need, and the number of time it will take to go, right?

244
00:35:14,000 --> 00:35:19,000
 But remember that this is still an assumption, right?

245
00:35:19,000 --> 00:35:21,000
 And this is called a Markov assumption.

246
00:35:21,000 --> 00:35:27,000
 Now, if I condition on just the previous value, it is called a first order Markov assumption.

247
00:35:27,000 --> 00:35:36,000
 If I choose to condition on the previous two values, it's called the second order Markov assumption, and so on, all right?

248
00:35:36,000 --> 00:35:39,000
 Okay, now here is my next group.

249
00:35:39,000 --> 00:35:47,000
 You should ask somebody who claims to be an expert on AI, is conditional independence the same as independence?

250
00:35:47,000 --> 00:35:56,000
 And, you know, the short answer is that the extra word suggests that it should not be conditional independence were the same as independence.

251
00:35:56,000 --> 00:36:00,000
 Why would I unnecessarily introduce the additional word conditional?

252
00:36:00,000 --> 00:36:02,000
 So, it does mean something, okay?

253
00:36:02,000 --> 00:36:06,000
 Now let's see what that something is, okay?

254
00:36:06,000 --> 00:36:14,000
 So, you see the Markov assumption makes a conditional independence function.

255
00:36:14,000 --> 00:36:21,000
 It does not say all the different values of x i are independent of each other.

256
00:36:21,000 --> 00:36:29,000
 It says x i is independent condition on the value of x i minus one, right?

257
00:36:29,000 --> 00:36:32,000
 That is a conditional independent assumption.

258
00:36:32,000 --> 00:36:34,000
 It's not an independent assumption.

259
00:36:34,000 --> 00:36:43,000
 Whereas if I make the IID assumption, that says every x i is independent of all the other x things, right?

260
00:36:43,000 --> 00:36:49,000
 So, this is an independent assumption, but the Markov assumption is a conditional independent assumption.

261
00:36:49,000 --> 00:36:57,000
 It says that x i becomes independent of all the other x j's once I know x i minus one, okay?

262
00:36:57,000 --> 00:37:05,000
 So, this is the difference between conditional independence and absolute or unconditional independence, okay?

263
00:37:05,000 --> 00:37:13,000
 So, this was our first definition of a generative model that it is a probabilistic program.

264
00:37:13,000 --> 00:37:21,000
 So, the second definition is hinted at by this, so you see the product over i.

265
00:37:21,000 --> 00:37:30,000
 So, what this means is that I am breaking up this big distribution into a product of simpler terms, right?

266
00:37:30,000 --> 00:37:37,000
 And the simpler terms in the first case are p x 1 into p x 2 into p x 3 and so on.

267
00:37:37,000 --> 00:37:46,000
 In the Markov case, it is p x 1, p x 2 given x 1, p x 3 given x 2, p x 4 given x 3 and so on.

268
00:37:46,000 --> 00:37:51,000
 So, each of these are called terms or factors, right?

269
00:37:51,000 --> 00:38:01,000
 So, what a generative model means is that it is a factorization of the entire joint distribution p x 1, x 2, x 3, x 4 up to x n

270
00:38:01,000 --> 00:38:09,000
 into a smaller, a product of a smaller set of terms such that it can be easily sampled from, okay?

271
00:38:09,000 --> 00:38:14,000
 And this is often done using a mechanism that is called a graphical model.

272
00:38:14,000 --> 00:38:16,000
 Why is it called a graphical model?

273
00:38:16,000 --> 00:38:21,000
 Because it is sort of represented pictorially using graphs, right?

274
00:38:21,000 --> 00:38:33,000
 So, here I have these n variables x 1 to x n, I am representing each of those using one node graph and so these nodes represent the random variables.

275
00:38:33,000 --> 00:38:42,000
 And the absence of edges between the nodes is an indication of conditional independence, right?

276
00:38:42,000 --> 00:38:49,000
 So, here for the iid case, you see that there are no edges at all between the different excites, right?

277
00:38:49,000 --> 00:38:54,000
 Which means that x 1 is independent of x 2, is independent of x 3 and so on.

278
00:38:54,000 --> 00:38:57,000
 Everything is independent of everything else, okay?

279
00:38:57,000 --> 00:38:59,000
 And why, what is that theta doing?

280
00:38:59,000 --> 00:39:07,000
 Well, all of them depend on the same value of theta, but theta is not really a random variable, it is a parameter of this.

281
00:39:07,000 --> 00:39:11,000
 Now, here is the first order Markov assumption.

282
00:39:11,000 --> 00:39:13,000
 Now, see what the edges are doing now.

283
00:39:13,000 --> 00:39:18,000
 So, x i, x 1 depends on nothing, right?

284
00:39:18,000 --> 00:39:24,000
 x 2 depends on that from which it has an income edge.

285
00:39:24,000 --> 00:39:34,000
 So, x 2 depends only on x 1, x 3 depends only on x 2, x n depends only on x n minus 1.

286
00:39:34,000 --> 00:39:40,000
 So, this pictorially represents the conditional independence or the conditional dependency.

287
00:39:40,000 --> 00:39:43,000
 So, this is the first order Markov assumption.

288
00:39:43,000 --> 00:39:53,000
 Before I go on to the next slide, I will want you to imagine in your heads, what would no assumption look like?

289
00:39:53,000 --> 00:40:00,000
 If I just try to represent the chain rule that makes no assumptions, what would the graph for that look like?

290
00:40:00,000 --> 00:40:08,000
 Take a second to think about it and then I will go to the next slide to show you the answer.

291
00:40:08,000 --> 00:40:12,000
 Okay, let us go to the next slide and see if you guessed it right.

292
00:40:12,000 --> 00:40:16,000
 So, x 1 has an edge to x 2, right?

293
00:40:16,000 --> 00:40:29,000
 x 2 has an incoming edge only from x 1, x 3 has an incoming edge from x 1 and x 2 and x n, x n has an incoming edge from everything before, right?

294
00:40:29,000 --> 00:40:34,000
 So, this says there is no conditional independence assumption, this is chain rule.

295
00:40:34,000 --> 00:40:41,000
 So, every variable depends on every other variable that came before it in the sequence, right?

296
00:40:41,000 --> 00:40:54,000
 So, this is another representation of a generative model because it breaks up the conditional distribution into smaller terms, one for each node, okay?

297
00:40:54,000 --> 00:41:05,000
 Now, if you look at these models, papers talking about VAE and things like that, you will all often seen these graphical representations.

298
00:41:05,000 --> 00:41:14,000
 So, again, the entire purpose of this lecture is when you see these things in the papers, you should have some idea what this means, right?

299
00:41:14,000 --> 00:41:20,000
 But you will not have a complete idea, but you should be able to go back to these slides here to understand what it would have meant.

300
00:41:20,000 --> 00:41:32,000
 So, often you will see this circle with this variable written in it with a box around it that says n, you know, what this really means is that this is a shorthand.

301
00:41:32,000 --> 00:41:36,000
 The picture on the left is equivalent to the picture on the right.

302
00:41:36,000 --> 00:41:45,000
 What it means is that whatever is inside that box, mark n, you should unfold that box n times.

303
00:41:45,000 --> 00:41:49,000
 So, it's like a for loop represented in these pictures, okay?

304
00:41:49,000 --> 00:41:55,000
 So, if you unfold that for loop n times, you will get the thing on the right, okay?

305
00:41:55,000 --> 00:41:59,000
 So, the left hand side in some sense is a shorthand for the right hand side.

306
00:41:59,000 --> 00:42:09,000
 And why do you need a shorthand? Well, it takes less time to draw it, less time and less space, so that's why it means shorthand, but it means the same thing, okay?

307
00:42:09,000 --> 00:42:21,000
 So, we have actually seen three different representations or definitions of a generative model and all of these three mean exactly the same thing.

308
00:42:21,000 --> 00:42:36,000
 The term on the left is the factorization representation where I have broken up a big distribution dx1.xn into a product of a small set of terms, right?

309
00:42:36,000 --> 00:42:43,000
 Or not a small set of terms into a product of terms, each of which is smaller than the original one, right?

310
00:42:43,000 --> 00:42:57,000
 In the middle, this is the graphical notation, right? And in the right, that is the probabilistic program notation, but all of these three things mean exactly the same thing, okay?

311
00:42:57,000 --> 00:43:10,000
 So, in some papers, you will see written in one way, in another paper, you will see written in a different way, but just so that you know whenever you come across this, all these three things mean the same thing.

312
00:43:10,000 --> 00:43:15,000
 Okay, now here is my next Google, we talked about a generative model, right?

313
00:43:15,000 --> 00:43:21,000
 Now, can a generative model be used for a non-generative task?

314
00:43:21,000 --> 00:43:29,000
 And this is not a ridiculous question, it can indeed be done and let me demystify what I'm saying here, okay?

315
00:43:29,000 --> 00:43:35,000
 So that you can bold this Google at other AI experts when you encounter them, okay?

316
00:43:35,000 --> 00:43:47,000
 So, what is a generative task? A generative task means that the task input is a label and the task output is data.

317
00:43:47,000 --> 00:43:56,000
 Please see if you can remember this, right? Generative task means the task input is a label or maybe nothing and the task output is data.

318
00:43:56,000 --> 00:44:05,000
 A generative model means that the model input is data, the model output is label.

319
00:44:05,000 --> 00:44:10,000
 So, is it possible to use a generative model for a generative task?

320
00:44:10,000 --> 00:44:28,000
 The answer is yes, if I could somehow invert the model for addressing this task and there is in fact such a notion of inversion of in a probabilistic model, okay?

321
00:44:28,000 --> 00:44:36,000
 And so just to give you an example, let's say this is a generative model for two class data.

322
00:44:36,000 --> 00:44:46,000
 What would this do? So, if I have two class data, let's say I want to generate data for cats and dogs or for spam and non-spam, right?

323
00:44:46,000 --> 00:44:57,000
 First, I would sample a label. If the label comes out to be spam, then I have a distribution for words that look like words in a spam in it, right?

324
00:44:57,000 --> 00:45:13,000
 And I will generate randomly generate a spam in it, right? Now, if I sample why the label and the value comes out to be a non-spam, then, you know, I have some standard distributions for how the words in a non-spam you may look like.

325
00:45:13,000 --> 00:45:16,000
 So, I will generate words following that distribution, right?

326
00:45:16,000 --> 00:45:30,000
 So, if I run this distribution or sample from this distribution often enough, what will I get? I will get, you know, as a data set where some instances are spam, some instances are non-spam, right?

327
00:45:30,000 --> 00:45:47,000
 So, this is a generative model for two class data. Now, could I use this model for predicting whether an email is spam or non-spam? That is a non-generative task, right?

328
00:45:47,000 --> 00:46:03,000
 I will be given an email and I have to classify using non-spam or spam. If I have this model given to me, can I use this model to accomplish that task?

329
00:46:03,000 --> 00:46:22,000
 And this is how to do it, okay? So, and this is one of the most important rules in all of science, okay? I would go to the extent of saying this is probably as important and should be as famous as equal to mc2, okay?

330
00:46:22,000 --> 00:46:42,000
 If you want to remember one rule from all of AI, that rule has to be Bayes' rule, okay? So, what does Bayes' rule say? But before getting into what Bayes' rule say, let us try to understand what Bayes' rule is trying to do.

331
00:46:42,000 --> 00:46:59,000
 If I have a model that generates possible effects given a cause, okay? So, think of y as the cause and x as the effects of a particular cause.

332
00:46:59,000 --> 00:47:15,000
 If I have a model that can generate possible effects given a cause, then can I use that model to predict possible causes given the effects, right?

333
00:47:15,000 --> 00:47:32,000
 This is what Bayes' rule looks to do and this is how it does it, just following the rules of probability and condition of probability. So, p y of x is written in terms of p y and p of x is a y, okay?

334
00:47:32,000 --> 00:47:49,000
 And of course, this needs to be normalized to make it a probability. So, the denominator is p of x, which is nothing but the sum over y, p of all values of y, p of y into p of x given y, right?

335
00:47:49,000 --> 00:48:05,000
 Now, this is called Bayes' rule and this is sort of one of the fundamental rules of generative AI, okay? I know there is some famous commentator who went down in history by saying something like, remember the name, what was the name?

336
00:48:05,000 --> 00:48:15,000
 I think he said, Carlos Greatweight or something like that. Of course, I have no pretentions of going down in history by saying this, but I will still say this, okay?

337
00:48:15,000 --> 00:48:25,000
 Remember the name, Thomas Bayes, that is more important than Carlos Greatweight when it comes to AI. Okay?

338
00:48:25,000 --> 00:48:42,000
 So, now, once we have understood Bayes' rule, let us come to what are the things that you would like to achieve if somebody wants you to create a generative model, right?

339
00:48:42,000 --> 00:48:54,000
 So, if you have a class for the generative model, then what are the functions or methods that you must implement for that class to qualify it as a generative model, right?

340
00:48:54,000 --> 00:49:00,000
 So, the first requirement is that it has to have a method called sample, right?

341
00:49:00,000 --> 00:49:10,000
 So, what that means is given the data distribution with known or estimated parameters, you have to be able to draw new synthetic data points from it.

342
00:49:10,000 --> 00:49:14,000
 And this has to be an efficient operation, right?

343
00:49:14,000 --> 00:49:24,000
 It cannot be something that takes quadratic, well, we will see actually models that take quadratic complexity, but ideally it should be efficient, okay?

344
00:49:24,000 --> 00:49:30,000
 The second is density estimation. What does that mean?

345
00:49:30,000 --> 00:49:46,000
 So, I am not asking you to draw a sample, but I am giving you a sample and I am asking you, under your generative model, what is the probability or score of seeing this particular output?

346
00:49:46,000 --> 00:49:52,000
 Could this output have come from your generative model? Can you give me a score, right?

347
00:49:52,000 --> 00:50:00,000
 So, if that score is low, it would mean that this value could not have come from this institution or is less likely to come from the institution.

348
00:50:00,000 --> 00:50:07,000
 If this value is high, then it means that, oh, I think this is really a value coming from this institution, right?

349
00:50:07,000 --> 00:50:15,000
 So, this is called density estimation and please understand that density estimation is very different from sampling.

350
00:50:15,000 --> 00:50:31,000
 When sampling, you are required to generate a new value. For density estimation, you will be given a value and you will be asked what is the probability of this particular value from this particular distribution, okay?

351
00:50:31,000 --> 00:50:49,000
 The next is inference, okay? And what this means, I will have to wait a little bit to explain what inference means, but in many tasks, there is something called a hidden variable, right?

352
00:50:49,000 --> 00:51:04,000
 For example, if you have to predict the hidden cause given the effect, right? For example, given the email, if you want to infer the label, that is called inference, right?

353
00:51:04,000 --> 00:51:12,000
 There are other examples of inference, we will come to that. So, a generative model should also be able to do inference, okay?

354
00:51:12,000 --> 00:51:24,000
 And inference has other uses. So, for example, creating a representation or encoding are also examples of inference, we will come to that, okay?

355
00:51:24,000 --> 00:51:36,000
 The next task is learning or parameter estimation, right? So, you should be able to give a sample of data points to this class, right?

356
00:51:36,000 --> 00:51:45,000
 And say that, look, I have seen these samples, can you estimate the parameters of your model from these sample data points, right?

357
00:51:45,000 --> 00:51:50,000
 And this is the learning or the parameter estimation ability.

358
00:51:50,000 --> 00:52:07,000
 And so, in some sense, what is required to do is, there is the data distribution, right? And there is the empirical distribution of the sample that is given to you.

359
00:52:07,000 --> 00:52:19,000
 And you are required to find that theta, which somehow minimizes the distance between the true distribution and the data distribution, right?

360
00:52:19,000 --> 00:52:26,000
 So, there is some notion of distance between distributions. So, there are many ways to measure distances between distributions.

361
00:52:26,000 --> 00:52:34,000
 Something like KL divergence is one particular way to minimize distribution, distance between distributions.

362
00:52:34,000 --> 00:52:40,000
 It is not the only one, but it is often used in machine learning for a particular reason, okay?

363
00:52:40,000 --> 00:52:59,000
 So, it turns out that minimizing KL divergence between the data distribution and the true distribution is equivalent to maximizing log likelihood under the data, of the data under the data distribution, right?

364
00:52:59,000 --> 00:53:13,000
 So, in some sense, KL maximizing or minimizing KL divergence is sort of used as the justification for why do you maximize likelihood of the data?

365
00:53:13,000 --> 00:53:24,000
 Why is that a good thing? Oh, it is a good thing to do because, you know, doing this is one way to minimize the distance between the two distributions and the outside.

366
00:53:24,000 --> 00:53:35,000
 Okay, alright. So, here is the next Google, right? So, can a deep model be non-neural?

367
00:53:35,000 --> 00:53:42,000
 I mean, if you ask a so-called AI expert, you would say, what are you even talking about? Of course not.

368
00:53:42,000 --> 00:53:51,000
 But you know what? The answer is a very trivial yes. You know, I did my PhD in 2006.

369
00:53:51,000 --> 00:54:04,000
 I completed my PhD in 2006 and my PhD was on a subject which was all about deep distributions, but had nothing to do with neural networks, okay?

370
00:54:04,000 --> 00:54:15,000
 So, what is really the difference between traditional and deep generative models? Okay, or I should have actually said neural generative models.

371
00:54:15,000 --> 00:54:26,000
 So, the difference really is in how you model the conditional probability terms in the factorized likelihood, right?

372
00:54:26,000 --> 00:54:38,000
 So, I had this p of x1, p of x2 given x1, p of x2 given x2. So, what kind of functions do you use for modeling these terms, right?

373
00:54:38,000 --> 00:54:47,000
 In traditional generative models, these would typically be standard distributions, right? What do I mean by standard distributions?

374
00:54:47,000 --> 00:55:01,000
 So, for categorical distributions or discrete distributions, these are, you know, geometric distributions, Poisson distribution, the Bernoulli distribution, the categorical distribution and so on, right?

375
00:55:01,000 --> 00:55:14,000
 Now, when it wants to continuous distributions, the exponential distribution, the Gaussian distribution, the Laplace distribution, students' distribution and things like that, okay?

376
00:55:14,000 --> 00:55:18,000
 This is how it used to be in traditional generative models.

377
00:55:18,000 --> 00:55:29,000
 Now, what modern generative models do is for the same distributions, instead of using these traditional distributions, which are the standard distributions,

378
00:55:29,000 --> 00:55:33,000
 what are used are deep neural networks.

379
00:55:33,000 --> 00:55:40,000
 Now, this is a huge, huge, huge dump in terms of ability, right? And why is that?

380
00:55:40,000 --> 00:55:51,000
 Because we know that a multi-layered neural network is able to approximate any distribution to arbitrary closeness, right?

381
00:55:51,000 --> 00:56:06,000
 So, if you want to learn complex distributions in the real world, then doing so with neural networks as your conditional distributions is significantly more powerful than using the standard distribution, right?

382
00:56:06,000 --> 00:56:23,000
 So, of course, what this requires is that you also have new breakthroughs in optimization algorithms, scalable implementations, et cetera, et cetera, which is exactly what has happened in the last, you know, 15 years or so, okay?

383
00:56:23,000 --> 00:56:28,000
 So now, what are the challenges in generative modeling?

384
00:56:28,000 --> 00:56:40,000
 Of course, we are trying to, so I said that the data is very high-dimensional, it has complex structure, and so, of course, it has a very complex high-dimensional distribution.

385
00:56:40,000 --> 00:56:48,000
 And modeling such complex high-dimensional distributions requires very complex distributions with many parameters.

386
00:56:48,000 --> 00:57:01,000
 And we know that unless you want to have so many parameters, you will have to make some simplifying assumptions such as Markov assumptions or IIT assumptions, which are unnatural and typically do not hold in the data.

387
00:57:01,000 --> 00:57:10,000
 And if you are using a large number of parameters, then you have to have large data volumes to prevent overfitting, right?

388
00:57:10,000 --> 00:57:18,000
 And working with such tremendously large data volumes involves a lot of very significant engineering challenges.

389
00:57:18,000 --> 00:57:31,000
 And then as models become deeper or longer, you have this problem of exploding or vanishing gradients, and that requires many, many innovations to get over those.

390
00:57:31,000 --> 00:57:45,000
 And finally, what we will see is that I sort of listed certain requirements from a generative model. What we will see is that satisfying all of those requirements simultaneously is very, very hard.

391
00:57:45,000 --> 00:57:52,000
 If you have to do well on some of those requirements, well, you will fall short on some of the other requirements and so on.

392
00:57:52,000 --> 00:57:59,000
 So there are different kinds of generative models, some of which do well on some requirements, but not so good on other requirements, etc.

393
00:57:59,000 --> 00:58:07,000
 And finally, right, there is a huge problem when it comes to generative AI.

394
00:58:07,000 --> 00:58:11,000
 How do I evaluate the quality of the output, right?

395
00:58:11,000 --> 00:58:26,000
 So when the nature of the output is simple, such as a yes or no value, one of five different values, you know, a particular real value, then it is possible to have a notion of a gold standard value.

396
00:58:26,000 --> 00:58:32,000
 And you can see how close your gold standard value is to the predicted value.

397
00:58:32,000 --> 00:58:37,000
 But if you are asking somebody to generate an essay, right?

398
00:58:37,000 --> 00:58:44,000
 I mean, how do you evaluate the quality of that essay? What is a gold standard? You cannot have a single gold standard for this, right?

399
00:58:44,000 --> 00:58:53,000
 So I will show you examples where, you know, things are not as subjective as an essay. Let us say I am asking you to generate a program, right?

400
00:58:53,000 --> 00:58:59,000
 So evaluating whether the program is a correct program or not is extremely difficult.

401
00:58:59,000 --> 00:59:10,000
 So evaluation of generative models is very, very hard, and this is one of the biggest problems when it comes to working with generative AI.

402
00:59:10,000 --> 00:59:22,000
 Now, one important thing that you will need to know when studying some of the later models, but I will keep it here so that you know that this is something important to learn is the notion of a transformation of a random period.

403
00:59:22,000 --> 00:59:35,000
 So let us say you have a random variable x with some PDFP of x and let us say you have another variable y, that is that y is a function x, right?

404
00:59:35,000 --> 00:59:43,000
 Now, what is, if I know the distribution of x, right, then what is the distribution of y, right?

405
00:59:43,000 --> 01:00:01,000
 And for that, what you need to do is written on the last line. So this is the transformation rule for, you know, scalar values and what you see on the right hand side, you see a differential with a modular sign, right?

406
01:00:01,000 --> 01:00:11,000
 And when it goes to vectors, right, this becomes something called a Jacobian matrix instead of particular value, right?

407
01:00:11,000 --> 01:00:18,000
 So this is just something that I will require you to keep in mind for more complex distributions.

408
01:00:18,000 --> 01:00:29,000
 But there's a problem, right, that computing the Jacobian is actually O of n cube, right, for an n by n matrix.

409
01:00:29,000 --> 01:00:45,000
 So when the dimensionality of your data, let's say your data is 1000 by 1000 or something like that, then computing the Jacobian naively would become O of 1000 cube, which as you can imagine is extremely computationally challenging, right?

410
01:00:45,000 --> 01:00:57,000
 So transformation of random variables is becomes crucial when working with generative AI, and this typically requires this transformation rule, which is computationally hard.

411
01:00:57,000 --> 01:01:06,000
 This is one important thing to keep in mind, and this is something that needs to be addressed when building complicated generative models, okay?

412
01:01:06,000 --> 01:01:19,000
 So this actually transformation of random variables is one way to go from a simple random variable to a more complex random variable.

413
01:01:19,000 --> 01:01:31,000
 What does that mean? So let's say I sample a value from Gaussian, and then I can do whatever deterministic transformation to the Gaussian value I want, and that can be a different random variable, right?

414
01:01:31,000 --> 01:01:36,000
 So now I have modeled a random variable, which is not a Gaussian anymore, right?

415
01:01:36,000 --> 01:01:48,000
 But I know how to sample that using Gaussian. This is one way to create more complex variables from simple variables or more complex distributions from simple distributions.

416
01:01:48,000 --> 01:01:54,000
 Another way to do that is called a mixture, okay?

417
01:01:54,000 --> 01:02:05,000
 So if I have two different distributions, then I can mix these two simple distributions to create a more complex distribution, right?

418
01:02:05,000 --> 01:02:13,000
 So as you know, a Gaussian distribution has a single mode, one mode, one peak, right?

419
01:02:13,000 --> 01:02:22,000
 So now if somebody, if you see some data which has three peaks, of course, you cannot model that using one Gaussian, right?

420
01:02:22,000 --> 01:02:25,000
 Because one Gaussian will never give you three peaks.

421
01:02:25,000 --> 01:02:30,000
 So how can you model a data which physically has three peaks using a Gaussian?

422
01:02:30,000 --> 01:02:38,000
 Well, the solution is actually to use three Gaussians and a mixture of three Gaussians.

423
01:02:38,000 --> 01:02:47,000
 If you mix three Gaussians in some interesting way, you will get a distribution that has three peaks.

424
01:02:47,000 --> 01:02:52,000
 And the typical notion of a mixture is called a complex composition, right?

425
01:02:52,000 --> 01:02:58,000
 So let's say I assign a weight corresponding to each of the distributions.

426
01:02:58,000 --> 01:03:07,000
 Let's say it is pi 1, pi 2, pi 3, and it is such that each of them is greater than 1 and the sum, sorry, greater than 0 and the sum to 1.

427
01:03:07,000 --> 01:03:14,000
 So for example, pi 1 could be 0.5, pi 2 could be 0.25, pi 3 could be 0.25, right?

428
01:03:14,000 --> 01:03:25,000
 And now when I create this distribution, which is pi 1 times the first Gaussian plus pi 2 times the second Gaussian plus pi 3 times the second Gaussian,

429
01:03:25,000 --> 01:03:30,000
 this magically becomes a distribution with three peaks, right?

430
01:03:30,000 --> 01:03:32,000
 And this is called a mixture distribution.

431
01:03:32,000 --> 01:03:38,000
 This is another way of creating more complex distributions from simple distributions, right?

432
01:03:38,000 --> 01:03:40,000
 So why am I talking about all this?

433
01:03:40,000 --> 01:03:49,000
 Because generative AI is really about modeling very complex distributions that are seen in the real world, right?

434
01:03:49,000 --> 01:03:54,000
 And so I am telling you what are the tools for creating complex distributions.

435
01:03:54,000 --> 01:04:03,000
 So if I have simple distributions, I can somehow put these simple distributions together, I can combine them in some way, I can augment them in some way, right?

436
01:04:03,000 --> 01:04:05,000
 To create more complex distributions.

437
01:04:05,000 --> 01:04:12,000
 So this transformation of random variables is one and the mixing of distributions is one.

438
01:04:12,000 --> 01:04:20,000
 And all through generative AI, you will see repeated news of these two techniques, okay?

439
01:04:20,000 --> 01:04:30,000
 All right, now we are all set to talk about our first class of generative models, which are auto-regressive models, all right?

440
01:04:30,000 --> 01:04:33,000
 So what are auto-regressive models?

441
01:04:33,000 --> 01:04:39,000
 You know, this is the simplest kind of generative model you can think of.

442
01:04:39,000 --> 01:04:45,000
 This just follows the chain rule of probability and nothing else, right?

443
01:04:45,000 --> 01:04:54,000
 So if I have to model a sequence of 10 words, then this will say model the first word, condition or nothing.

444
01:04:54,000 --> 01:05:04,000
 Model the second word, condition of the first word, model the third word, condition of the first two words, model the fourth word, condition of the first three words and so on.

445
01:05:04,000 --> 01:05:07,000
 This is exactly the auto-regressive model.

446
01:05:07,000 --> 01:05:11,000
 It makes no conditional independence assumptions at all.

447
01:05:11,000 --> 01:05:16,000
 So in some sense, this is the simplest graphical model you can think of, okay?

448
01:05:16,000 --> 01:05:20,000
 But there is a price for simplicity.

449
01:05:20,000 --> 01:05:21,000
 What is the price?

450
01:05:21,000 --> 01:05:22,000
 There are many prices.

451
01:05:22,000 --> 01:05:30,000
 In fact, the first is that there has to be some linear ordering of the variables, right?

452
01:05:30,000 --> 01:05:35,000
 When you model using an auto-regressive model.

453
01:05:35,000 --> 01:05:41,000
 So this linear ordering is natural in some cases.

454
01:05:41,000 --> 01:05:44,000
 So it is natural in terms of time series.

455
01:05:44,000 --> 01:05:47,000
 It is natural in terms of line, natural language.

456
01:05:47,000 --> 01:05:53,000
 You could say it is even natural in terms of code because anyway you write code from left to right.

457
01:05:53,000 --> 01:05:56,000
 So code is also a sequence of characters, right?

458
01:05:56,000 --> 01:06:04,000
 However, if you have to generate images, can you form a linear ordering of all the pixels in the image?

459
01:06:04,000 --> 01:06:06,000
 Very certainly not natural, right?

460
01:06:06,000 --> 01:06:12,000
 So there is no one linear ordering of the pixels in an image, but there are ways to do it.

461
01:06:12,000 --> 01:06:17,000
 For example, you could use something like a raster scan, which goes from left to right and top to bottom.

462
01:06:17,000 --> 01:06:22,000
 And then you can get one particular linear ordering of the pixels and images.

463
01:06:22,000 --> 01:06:30,000
 In fact, if you have to generate images using an auto-regressive model, this is an unnatural thing that you have to do, okay?

464
01:06:30,000 --> 01:06:34,000
 But what is the bigger problem, right?

465
01:06:34,000 --> 01:06:42,000
 We have already seen that if you do not make any conditional independence assumptions, right?

466
01:06:42,000 --> 01:06:49,000
 Then the number of parameters that you will need to use becomes exponential.

467
01:06:49,000 --> 01:06:58,000
 In fact, if you think about the distribution of the nth random variable, depending on the first n minus one random variable,

468
01:06:58,000 --> 01:07:05,000
 that distribution is going to be different depending on the specific values that the first n minus one random variable take, right?

469
01:07:05,000 --> 01:07:11,000
 And we have seen that trying to do this naively requires an exponential number of parameters.

470
01:07:11,000 --> 01:07:15,000
 It also requires exponential space to store and so on.

471
01:07:15,000 --> 01:07:23,000
 So we have to have a way somehow to reduce the number of parameters that is required.

472
01:07:23,000 --> 01:07:28,000
 And this is typically done using a neural network, okay?

473
01:07:28,000 --> 01:07:29,000
 How do we do that?

474
01:07:29,000 --> 01:07:36,000
 So let's say our random variables x1 to xn are binary random variables, right?

475
01:07:36,000 --> 01:07:38,000
 Each of them take value 0, 1.

476
01:07:38,000 --> 01:07:43,000
 So what is typically the distribution that we use to model binary random variables?

477
01:07:43,000 --> 01:07:45,000
 It's a Bernoulli distribution.

478
01:07:45,000 --> 01:07:49,000
 And what is the parameter of a Bernoulli distribution?

479
01:07:49,000 --> 01:07:52,000
 It's a probability of head, rho, right?

480
01:07:52,000 --> 01:08:03,000
 So what I will do is, in every case, I will make rho a function of all the previous values of x, right?

481
01:08:03,000 --> 01:08:14,000
 So I will get a single value, which is the probability of head, which depends on the specific values taken by x1 to xi minus one.

482
01:08:14,000 --> 01:08:23,000
 If I have such a function, right, then I have sort of gotten around this requirement of having exponential space, right?

483
01:08:23,000 --> 01:08:27,000
 All that I have to store is just this function, right?

484
01:08:27,000 --> 01:08:34,000
 And then I have to figure out how I can model this function with significantly fewer number of parameters, right?

485
01:08:34,000 --> 01:08:37,000
 Now what about categorical random variables?

486
01:08:37,000 --> 01:08:41,000
 Categorical random variables require multiple parameters.

487
01:08:41,000 --> 01:08:49,000
 So the nature of the parameters that we need is, you know, probability of the first one, probability of the second one, probability of the third one.

488
01:08:49,000 --> 01:08:52,000
 And this is typically done using a softmax function.

489
01:08:52,000 --> 01:09:03,000
 So I need a softmax function that goes from xi minus x1 to xi minus minus one, and it gives you a set of values that are between 0 and 1, okay?

490
01:09:03,000 --> 01:09:09,000
 Now, if I have a real value random variable, I know I need to model that using Gaussian.

491
01:09:09,000 --> 01:09:13,000
 For each value of i, right, I will have a different Gaussian.

492
01:09:13,000 --> 01:09:14,000
 How can I do that?

493
01:09:14,000 --> 01:09:23,000
 I will make the mean and the variance of that particular Gaussian a function of all the previously seen values, right?

494
01:09:23,000 --> 01:09:27,000
 So, and what kind of specific functions I can use, right?

495
01:09:27,000 --> 01:09:35,000
 Oh, before I come to that, then how can I sort of, what are the things that we have to do with a generative model to remember?

496
01:09:35,000 --> 01:09:39,000
 So the first few things are scoring and sampling, okay?

497
01:09:39,000 --> 01:09:44,000
 Now, when it comes to scoring, you know, it's easy, right?

498
01:09:44,000 --> 01:09:52,000
 So I know that I have to score the probability for each xi and I just have to multiply them, right?

499
01:09:52,000 --> 01:09:57,000
 Or if it's a log, then I have to do the log of each of them and add them up, right?

500
01:09:57,000 --> 01:09:59,000
 And how do I score each of them?

501
01:09:59,000 --> 01:10:07,000
 Each of them is a Bernoulli or a Gaussian or a categorical and I have the parameter values so I can easily score it.

502
01:10:07,000 --> 01:10:14,000
 And the more interesting thing is that I can do all of this in a parallelizable way.

503
01:10:14,000 --> 01:10:19,000
 I don't have to wait to score px1 before I can score p of x2.

504
01:10:19,000 --> 01:10:24,000
 I can score p of x3 at the same time as p of x1 and p of x2.

505
01:10:24,000 --> 01:10:27,000
 So this is parallelizable in a straight forward way.

506
01:10:27,000 --> 01:10:33,000
 However, during testing, it is going to be slow.

507
01:10:33,000 --> 01:10:34,000
 Why is that?

508
01:10:34,000 --> 01:10:43,000
 Because unless I have generated the value of x1, I do not know the parameter value for x2, right?

509
01:10:43,000 --> 01:10:45,000
 Because it depends on the value of x1.

510
01:10:45,000 --> 01:10:47,000
 So I cannot generate x2.

511
01:10:47,000 --> 01:10:53,000
 If I have to generate x3, I need to know the parameter values for the distribution for x3.

512
01:10:53,000 --> 01:10:58,000
 It depends on the specific values of x1 and x2, right?

513
01:10:58,000 --> 01:11:04,000
 So my generation or sampling has to proceed step by step, right?

514
01:11:04,000 --> 01:11:10,000
 This is the biggest problem of auto regression once, okay?

515
01:11:10,000 --> 01:11:12,000
 Now, what about learning?

516
01:11:12,000 --> 01:11:15,000
 You know, learning is also straightforward.

517
01:11:15,000 --> 01:11:22,000
 If I know how to score the log likelihood, I can, you know, you already know about gradient descent from your previous lectures.

518
01:11:22,000 --> 01:11:30,000
 So I can form mini batches and I can calculate the gradient using mini batches and I can take steps using the gradient.

519
01:11:30,000 --> 01:11:33,000
 So learning is easy, right?

520
01:11:33,000 --> 01:11:36,000
 So I just said it can be a function.

521
01:11:36,000 --> 01:11:41,000
 Now, let us look at what kind of functions these can be, right?

522
01:11:41,000 --> 01:11:48,000
 So let's take the simplest case where, you know, this is Bernoulli, right?

523
01:11:48,000 --> 01:11:57,000
 So I need to have a single probability of head, which is a function of x1 to xn minus 1.

524
01:11:57,000 --> 01:12:04,000
 So how can I take arbitrary values of x1 to xn minus 1 and get a value between 0 and 1?

525
01:12:04,000 --> 01:12:09,000
 Well, I just first make a linear combination and then I pass it through a sigmoid.

526
01:12:09,000 --> 01:12:13,000
 In fact, what you see on the right is actually called a perceptron, right?

527
01:12:13,000 --> 01:12:19,000
 So I just create a perceptron, right? And that will give me a value for p of head.

528
01:12:19,000 --> 01:12:24,000
 So I just have a perceptron for each node x of i, right?

529
01:12:24,000 --> 01:12:26,000
 And this is a single-layer neural network.

530
01:12:26,000 --> 01:12:34,000
 So I have actually created a neural autoregressive model using a linear number of parameters, okay?

531
01:12:34,000 --> 01:12:42,000
 Now, if I want, I could make this more complex because I know that a linear neural network is not the most powerful neural network.

532
01:12:42,000 --> 01:12:49,000
 So I could use a multi-layered perceptron using a single perceptron, right?

533
01:12:49,000 --> 01:12:57,000
 And if I do that, for each p of head, I get, let's say, a two-layer neural network, right?

