01:21:29,000 --> 01:21:34,000
 And it cannot learn representations in an unsupervised way.

614
01:21:34,000 --> 01:21:45,000
 Okay, so in the little bit of time that we have left, what I would want to tell you about very briefly is about latent variable models.

615
01:21:45,000 --> 01:21:49,000
 So what are latent variables?

616
01:21:49,000 --> 01:21:57,000
 So data often contains unrecorded variables, which are explained in the operator.

617
01:21:57,000 --> 01:22:02,000
 For example, I talked about recording the heights of people in a population.

618
01:22:02,000 --> 01:22:08,000
 Let's say you did this exercise for all the population of the world.

619
01:22:08,000 --> 01:22:12,000
 Now here is something that was not recorded in data.

620
01:22:12,000 --> 01:22:16,000
 Let's say the ethnicities of the people.

621
01:22:16,000 --> 01:22:22,000
 And as we know, there is a very close correlation between ethnicity and height.

622
01:22:22,000 --> 01:22:27,000
 People from certain ethnicities are much taller than people from certain other ethnicities.

623
01:22:27,000 --> 01:22:30,000
 But this person never recorded.

624
01:22:30,000 --> 01:22:32,000
 Only the heights were recorded.

625
01:22:32,000 --> 01:22:46,000
 So if Xi be the observed height of the i-th person, I will denote Zi as the unobserved or the latent ethnic group of the i-th person.

626
01:22:46,000 --> 01:22:56,000
 So is there some way that I could recover given Xi, can I create this encoding vector Zi?

627
01:22:56,000 --> 01:23:03,000
 What would be the meaning of the encoding vector instead of the heights and recording their ethnicities?

628
01:23:03,000 --> 01:23:11,000
 So you could think of this as some kind of an explanation or an encoding of my original data.

629
01:23:11,000 --> 01:23:27,000
 So the task of encoding or latent variable inference is to recover such hidden explanations or underlying facts about the data that are not immediately apparent.

630
01:23:27,000 --> 01:23:35,000
 And I would like to get to this encoding without requiring any observed labels.

631
01:23:35,000 --> 01:23:43,000
 For example, nobody is going to give me training data for what the ethnicities are of the people whose heights were recorded.

632
01:23:43,000 --> 01:23:44,000
 That was not done.

633
01:23:44,000 --> 01:23:48,000
 And I cannot ask these people to go back and also record their ethnicities.

634
01:23:48,000 --> 01:23:49,000
 It has already been done.

635
01:23:49,000 --> 01:23:51,000
 That recording cannot be done anymore.

636
01:23:51,000 --> 01:23:53,000
 Now what can I do?

637
01:23:53,000 --> 01:23:59,000
 So here, as you can see, is that I have changed the model a little bit.

638
01:23:59,000 --> 01:24:06,000
 This is very, very similar to the model that I showed to you about classification, if you remember.

639
01:24:06,000 --> 01:24:13,000
 So earlier the label Y corresponded to a particular class, spam or non-spam.

640
01:24:13,000 --> 01:24:20,000
 Here, Zi refers to the ethnicity, maybe some different values of ethnicities.

641
01:24:20,000 --> 01:24:22,000
 There are k possible values.

642
01:24:22,000 --> 01:24:26,000
 So Zi takes one of k possible values.

643
01:24:26,000 --> 01:24:32,000
 The difference, if you will notice, is that Xi is shaded.

644
01:24:32,000 --> 01:24:37,000
 Shaded indicates that these values are observed in the data.

645
01:24:37,000 --> 01:24:39,000
 Zi is not shaded.

646
01:24:39,000 --> 01:24:44,000
 What it means is that Zi, Zi are not observed in the data.

647
01:24:44,000 --> 01:24:52,000
 And I will show you now what a huge difference this makes in terms of working with this model.

648
01:24:52,000 --> 01:25:01,000
 But just to give you some other names that you may come across, this kind of a model is often called a mixture model.

649
01:25:01,000 --> 01:25:03,000
 And why is this called a mixture model?

650
01:25:03,000 --> 01:25:10,000
 Because you can imagine that there is one Gaussian distribution for the heights of people in one particular ethnicity.

651
01:25:10,000 --> 01:25:14,000
 So if there are k different ethnicities, there are k Gaussian distributions.

652
01:25:14,000 --> 01:25:21,000
 And this entire distribution is a mixture of this k distribution.

653
01:25:21,000 --> 01:25:25,000
 So that is why this is called a Gaussian mixture model.

654
01:25:25,000 --> 01:25:27,000
 Now, what is the problem?

655
01:25:27,000 --> 01:25:34,000
 So if you have to learn this model, then I need to calculate the gradients with respect to theta.

656
01:25:34,000 --> 01:25:43,000
 Now, the problem is when I try to calculate the p, the Zs were not observed.

657
01:25:43,000 --> 01:25:47,000
 So I only know the distribution of t x, z.

658
01:25:47,000 --> 01:25:50,000
 I do not know the distribution x.

659
01:25:50,000 --> 01:26:01,000
 So to go to the distribution x, from the distribution of p x, z, I need to either sum or integrate out over possible sets.

660
01:26:01,000 --> 01:26:07,000
 And this integral or sum is there inside the logarithm.

661
01:26:07,000 --> 01:26:11,000
 And this makes the logarithm intractable.

662
01:26:11,000 --> 01:26:15,000
 We do not know how to do this mathematically.

663
01:26:15,000 --> 01:26:21,000
 So one way to get around this is something called an E n algorithm.

664
01:26:21,000 --> 01:26:26,000
 And the insight for the E n algorithm is very simple.

665
01:26:26,000 --> 01:26:35,000
 So if somebody had told, if somebody had guessed to begin with, what are the ethnicities of the different people?

666
01:26:35,000 --> 01:26:42,000
 So when z is known, then the likelihood is very, very easy to complete.

667
01:26:42,000 --> 01:26:49,000
 So maybe I could start off with some guess about what are the ethnicities of the different people.

668
01:26:49,000 --> 01:26:58,000
 So using that guess, I will get some value of the parameter theta, which are the means and the variances of the different gaussians.

669
01:26:58,000 --> 01:27:08,000
 And then starting from that guess, I will try to reclassify each person according to his or her ethnicity.

670
01:27:08,000 --> 01:27:23,000
 Because now I have a classification model. So what the E step does is that it computes the posterior distribution over the different ethnicities using the currently estimated parameters theta.

671
01:27:23,000 --> 01:27:27,000
 So these two steps are called the E steps and the M steps.

672
01:27:27,000 --> 01:27:33,000
 So the E step, I am going to re-guess the values of ethnicities of everybody.

673
01:27:33,000 --> 01:27:46,000
 And then in the follow up M step, using the latest guess, I am going to maximize the value of the parameters or the like, well, maximize the likelihood and find the corresponding value of the parameters.

674
01:27:46,000 --> 01:28:00,000
 So it is actually magical that this algorithm that sounds quite ad hoc is actually an algorithm that has very nice guarantees in theory.

675
01:28:00,000 --> 01:28:06,000
 This is called an iterative lower bound maximizing algorithm that converges in theory.

676
01:28:06,000 --> 01:28:12,000
 But the pitfall with all things theory is that it converges to a local maximum.

677
01:28:12,000 --> 01:28:21,000
 And if your likelihood function is multimodal, EM is extremely notorious for getting caught in local maximum.

678
01:28:21,000 --> 01:28:31,000
 So what is done in practice is that you would run EM over and over again, the different starting points, and then, you know, that you hope that that lets you get beyond local maximum.

679
01:28:31,000 --> 01:28:33,000
 Okay.

