01:29:56,000 --> 01:30:06,000
 So you can imagine that that is so much more powerful in terms of what it can present compared to a finite mixture of Gaussians.

688
01:30:06,000 --> 01:30:21,000
 So what is the model now? So now instead of sampling z from a Bernoulli distribution, I will sample z also from a Gaussian distribution with mean 0 and identity covariance.

689
01:30:21,000 --> 01:30:30,000
 Okay. And then from that Gaussian, I'm going to, so once I have the parameter of the Gaussian, I will sample the value from the Gaussian.

690
01:30:30,000 --> 01:30:41,000
 So I have an infinite number of Gaussians now for generating the x i. So this, you won't believe this, but this is the variational order in quotas.

691
01:30:41,000 --> 01:30:48,000
 I do not know if anybody has talked to you about variational order in quotas like this, but this is the variational order in quotas.

692
01:30:48,000 --> 01:30:54,000
 So where is the magic in all this? Well, the magic is not in defining the model.

693
01:30:54,000 --> 01:31:01,000
 It's actually in coming up with all the other algorithms for this particular model. Okay.

694
01:31:01,000 --> 01:31:12,000
 So why is this hard? As I've already told you, for phase root for inference, if I have to figure out the z given the x is, that is the encoding tough.

695
01:31:12,000 --> 01:31:16,000
 I know the x, I want to find out the z, that is encoding.

696
01:31:16,000 --> 01:31:27,000
 But the z given x requires this denominator. Right. And I've already said that this denominator is intractable for high dimensional set.

697
01:31:27,000 --> 01:31:38,000
 Because if it is discrete, this summation is going to be a huge summation. If it isn't continuous, then you know, this analytical integral may not exist.

698
01:31:38,000 --> 01:31:44,000
 So I do not know how to calculate the denominator. So how am I going to proceed?

699
01:31:44,000 --> 01:31:55,000
 So I am going to gloss over the following slides, because clearly there isn't enough time to cover all of this in detail, but I will just give you a very high level idea.

700
01:31:55,000 --> 01:32:01,000
 So what is used is something called variational approximation.

701
01:32:01,000 --> 01:32:12,000
 So if I use the true distribution t of beta, then doing inference using the true distribution is not possible. It is intractable.

702
01:32:12,000 --> 01:32:26,000
 So I will replace or approximate the true distribution with some other Q of phi, such that doing inference with the simpler distribution is simpler.

703
01:32:26,000 --> 01:32:31,000
 It is more tractable using Q than using the original distribution.

704
01:32:31,000 --> 01:32:41,000
 So this Q is called the variational distribution and the parameters of this variation distribution are inside, are the parameters of phi.

705
01:32:41,000 --> 01:32:55,000
 And then I will sort of move the log inside the integral, and we know we cannot do that. If you do that, it does not remain an equality anymore, but it becomes an inequality.

706
01:32:55,000 --> 01:33:09,000
 And this inequality is called tension inequality. So instead of giving you the likelihood, it actually gives you a lower bound on the likelihood, which is called the evidence lower bound or the elbow.

707
01:33:09,000 --> 01:33:20,000
 And very interestingly, there is lower bound with style, when this Q is the same as the original distribution, then this is a perfect equality. There is no approximation.

708
01:33:20,000 --> 01:33:27,000
 But if you use some other distribution which is not Q, not P, then it becomes a lower bound.

709
01:33:27,000 --> 01:33:45,000
 And so what you do with variational learning is that you will see a similarity with EM. So you use inference where you actually optimize the lower bound with respect to phi.

710
01:33:45,000 --> 01:33:54,000
 You are trying to find the best phi, such that this Q distribution comes as close as possible to the P distribution.

711
01:33:54,000 --> 01:34:05,000
 And then once you have a new Q distribution, you just proceed just as before, like the EM algorithm with this Q distribution, and you iterate over this.

712
01:34:05,000 --> 01:34:16,000
 And this is called variational learning. I am sorry I do not have time to get into more details, but I hope I have given you some idea of how variational learning.

713
01:34:16,000 --> 01:34:27,000
 Now the problem, again, the reason I am giving you these terms is that you will hear these terms often when you learn about, or what you hear about, you know, VAEs.

714
01:34:27,000 --> 01:34:35,000
 So I want to give you an idea of what these different terms mean, so that you can connect with this lecture.

715
01:34:35,000 --> 01:34:46,000
 So the problem is that you will have to compute an expectation with respect to this Q distribution when you are trying to do inference.

716
01:34:46,000 --> 01:34:52,000
 And we know that this analytical expectation is not always possible.

717
01:34:52,000 --> 01:35:05,000
 So one way to approximate an analytical expectation is to do expectation using sample. This is called Monte Carlo execution.

718
01:35:05,000 --> 01:35:18,000
 So I just take some sample values at fx, I take the average, sorry, I take some sample values of x, I take calculate the value of fx for all of those sample values, I take the average.

719
01:35:18,000 --> 01:35:29,000
 And believe it or not, when my number of samples is large, then the sample average is a very close approximation of the expectation.

720
01:35:29,000 --> 01:35:43,000
 This is called Monte Carlo execution. And this is the idea that is used in stochastic variation inference as opposed to, sorry, as compared to perfect or exact variation.

721
01:35:43,000 --> 01:35:52,000
 And then the notion is that stochastic variation inference leads to very large variance when computing the expectation.

722
01:35:52,000 --> 01:35:57,000
 So, you remember I talked about transformation of random variables.

723
01:35:57,000 --> 01:36:06,000
 So in VAE, there is this beautiful transformation that is done, which is called the reparameterization trick.

724
01:36:06,000 --> 01:36:12,000
 So to model Q of z, I will do something very straightforward.

725
01:36:12,000 --> 01:36:22,000
 I will first draw a sample epsilon from a simple distribution, which is a normal distribution with mean 0 and variance 1.

726
01:36:22,000 --> 01:36:29,000
 And then I will do a deterministic transformation to the simple normal variable.

727
01:36:29,000 --> 01:36:35,000
 I will do z equal to mu plus sigma e.

728
01:36:35,000 --> 01:36:47,000
 Now, the beautiful thing that happens is that now I don't have to take expectation over z, because z is a deterministic variable of a random value which is epsilon.

729
01:36:47,000 --> 01:36:52,000
 So I just need to take the expectation over epsilon instead of over z.

730
01:36:52,000 --> 01:36:59,000
 And this significantly reduces the sample variance. This is known as the reparameterization trick.

731
01:36:59,000 --> 01:37:14,000
 So, I want you to notice, I have talked about VAEs for so long, I have not talked about neural networks anywhere.

732
01:37:14,000 --> 01:37:17,000
 There are no neural networks so far.

733
01:37:17,000 --> 01:37:22,000
 Now, I will tell you when neural networks play a role.

734
01:37:22,000 --> 01:37:29,000
 So this is very, very important. Neural networks are not essential for deep learning.

735
01:37:29,000 --> 01:37:32,000
 You can have deep models without neural networks.

736
01:37:32,000 --> 01:37:41,000
 But when you plug neural networks into deep models, they become significantly more powerful.

737
01:37:41,000 --> 01:37:53,000
 So, I have the regular VAE just as before, but what I will do is for these are the parameters of the Gaussian distribution.

738
01:37:53,000 --> 01:38:01,000
 And I will use neural networks for modeling mu of z and sigma of z.

739
01:38:01,000 --> 01:38:05,000
 And I will use multi-layered perceptron for this.

740
01:38:05,000 --> 01:38:11,000
 So this is the first place where neural networks are used in VAEs.

741
01:38:11,000 --> 01:38:23,000
 The second place where neural networks are used is that this variational distribution which is mu of z given x.

742
01:38:23,000 --> 01:38:28,000
 I will use a neural network for this variational distribution.

743
01:38:28,000 --> 01:38:35,000
 And this is called the recognition model or the encoder or the inference network.

744
01:38:35,000 --> 01:38:40,000
 And I will use this one inference network that is shared between training and test.

745
01:38:40,000 --> 01:38:44,000
 And this is called amortized variation methods.

746
01:38:44,000 --> 01:38:48,000
 So, you see how I broke this into two parts.

747
01:38:48,000 --> 01:38:54,000
 You can define variational auto-encoders without getting into neural networks at all.

748
01:38:54,000 --> 01:39:05,000
 And then you can talk about how neural networks can use to make variational auto-encoders more powerful, more efficient.

749
01:39:05,000 --> 01:39:08,000
 Here is another quick question.

750
01:39:08,000 --> 01:39:14,000
 So what can you do with a variational auto-encoder that you cannot do with a standard auto-encoder?

751
01:39:14,000 --> 01:39:21,000
 And the answer is that you cannot generate new samples using an auto-encoder.

752
01:39:21,000 --> 01:39:28,000
 Because an auto-encoder uses a deterministic encoder and a deterministic decoder.

753
01:39:28,000 --> 01:39:33,000
 It cannot generate a new value of x.

754
01:39:33,000 --> 01:39:36,000
 You can try to do it. There are some ways for those don't work.

755
01:39:36,000 --> 01:39:42,000
 So if you want to generate new values of x, you cannot do it using auto-encoder.

756
01:39:42,000 --> 01:39:50,000
 So an auto-encoder is not a generating model, but VAE is a generating model.

757
01:39:50,000 --> 01:39:54,000
 So here is the VAE summary.

758
01:39:54,000 --> 01:39:58,000
 It learns latent representations in an unsupervised way.

759
01:39:58,000 --> 01:40:02,000
 So it uses simple distributions to construct complex ones.

760
01:40:02,000 --> 01:40:07,000
 And it uses inference networks to encode and it allows for efficient sampling.

761
01:40:07,000 --> 01:40:10,000
 But what are the trade-offs?

762
01:40:10,000 --> 01:40:14,000
 You are only able to obtain a lower bound on the density.

763
01:40:14,000 --> 01:40:19,000
 You cannot calculate the exact density and learning is difficult.

764
01:40:19,000 --> 01:40:23,000
 And there are many, many variants of the VAE.

765
01:40:23,000 --> 01:40:25,000
 I talked about only the basis framework.

766
01:40:25,000 --> 01:40:29,000
 These are info VAEs, VAE, RA, NNs and so on and so forth.

767
01:40:29,000 --> 01:40:40,000
 By the way, Dali uses VAE for the image part and then it has a transformer for the text.

768
01:40:40,000 --> 01:40:48,000
 I am really not sure we have much time to get into GANs, but I will give you a brief overview.

769
01:40:48,000 --> 01:40:54,000
 So far, we have talked about learning with likelihood.

770
01:40:54,000 --> 01:41:03,000
 Now the problem with this is that high likelihood does not always generate good samples.

771
01:41:03,000 --> 01:41:11,000
 And I could get into an entire chapter on this, but I don't have time to get into that.

772
01:41:11,000 --> 01:41:20,000
 But the point that I am making is that high likelihood does not always mean a good quality sample.

773
01:41:20,000 --> 01:41:25,000
 You can have more samples with high likelihood and you can have great samples with more likelihood.

774
01:41:25,000 --> 01:41:33,000
 So can we distinguish between distributions without having an explicit notion of likelihood?

775
01:41:33,000 --> 01:41:42,000
 If somebody only gave me samples from two different distributions and I have no notion of their likelihood,

776
01:41:42,000 --> 01:41:49,000
 can I be able to say whether these two sets of samples came from the same distribution or from different distribution?

777
01:41:49,000 --> 01:41:53,000
 And I need efficient computation for doing this.

778
01:41:53,000 --> 01:41:56,000
 So this is really some kind of a hypothesis test.

779
01:41:56,000 --> 01:42:03,000
 I have one set of samples S1 which came from distribution P, sorry this should have been an S2, this is a typo.

780
01:42:03,000 --> 01:42:09,000
 I have a second set of samples S2 which came from a different distribution Q, unknown to me.

781
01:42:09,000 --> 01:42:18,000
 And what I am supposed to decide is whether P and Q are the same distribution or different distribution.

782
01:42:18,000 --> 01:42:28,000
 Now, the idea of generative adversarial networks is that I will compare using a classifier.

783
01:42:28,000 --> 01:42:38,000
 If it is hard for a classifier to decide whether a sample came from P or it came from Q,

784
01:42:38,000 --> 01:42:43,000
 then these samples must be really close to each other.

785
01:42:43,000 --> 01:42:51,000
 So I will learn a discriminator model D5 to distinguish between the two sets of samples.

786
01:42:51,000 --> 01:43:04,000
 And if you are familiar with cross entropy loss, you will recognize what I have written below as some form of the cross entropy loss in terms of the discriminator model.

787
01:43:04,000 --> 01:43:17,000
 Now, so what I have just said is that I will use the notion of a classifier to decide whether two sets of samples come from the same distribution or a different distribution.

788
01:43:17,000 --> 01:43:20,000
 And this is called a discriminator model.

789
01:43:20,000 --> 01:43:37,000
 So if the discriminator is easily fooled, then I know that the two distributions are close to each other and if the discriminator is not easily fooled, then I would think that the two distributions are sufficient to differentiate each other.

790
01:43:37,000 --> 01:43:52,000
 Now, I will bring in the notion of a generator. So what is the task of a generator? The task of a generator is to generate samples that will fool the discriminator.

791
01:43:52,000 --> 01:44:01,000
 If I am able to fool the descriptor, then I must be generating samples according to the two distributions.

792
01:44:01,000 --> 01:44:12,000
 If the discriminator is easily able to say that no, no, the samples that you are generating are not like the samples from the two distribution, then I know that my samples are poor quality.

793
01:44:12,000 --> 01:44:26,000
 But if the discriminator is unable to distinguish between the samples that I generate or a model generates and the samples from the two distribution, then I must be generating good quality samples.

794
01:44:26,000 --> 01:44:38,000
 So I will try to learn a generator that fools the discriminator to the maximum extent possible. And this is a min-max optimization problem.

795
01:44:38,000 --> 01:44:47,000
 And this is really what a GAN looks to do. So this is the framework for GAN learning. I will sample m training data points from D.

796
01:44:47,000 --> 01:44:56,000
 I will sample m synthetic data points from V theta. I will update the discriminator parameters using gradient ascent.

797
01:44:56,000 --> 01:45:06,000
 So notice the ascent versus descent because in one case I am trying to maximize, in another case I am trying to minimize. This is a min-max optimization problem.

798
01:45:06,000 --> 01:45:12,000
 And you repeat this for a fixed number of epochs or until convergence and this is GAN learning.

799
01:45:12,000 --> 01:45:27,000
 Again, note that I have not talked about a neural network anywhere so far. So GANs or the basic concept of GANs does have nothing to do with neural networks.

800
01:45:27,000 --> 01:45:37,000
 But what are the issues with GANs? No well-defined density of x. So you cannot score. Learning latent representations is not straightforward.

801
01:45:37,000 --> 01:45:45,000
 There is a problem for mode collapse and because of the min-max nature, the optimization is typically unstable.

802
01:45:45,000 --> 01:45:52,000
 Now let us come to how neural architectures can be used to build better GANs.

803
01:45:52,000 --> 01:45:59,000
 So you have learned the trick so far. What you will say is look, you have talked about the generator and discriminator.

804
01:45:59,000 --> 01:46:09,000
 You ask, allow me to use neural network. I will use a neural network for both the generator and the discriminator and that will be a neural GAN and I will tell you that you are absolutely right.

805
01:46:09,000 --> 01:46:17,000
 So use the right kind of neural network depending on the nature of your data and you will have a GAN for that kind of data.

806
01:46:17,000 --> 01:46:29,000
 So if you are dealing with images, then use a convolution architecture and so DC GAN style GANs, these are GANs for images.

807
01:46:29,000 --> 01:46:43,000
 So if you are trying to generate text data, then use a transformer and an attention-based architecture for the generator and that will give you the best quality GAN for text.

808
01:46:43,000 --> 01:46:50,000
 And in fact, there are many variants of GANs. The bi-GAN is a variant of GAN that learns representation.

809
01:46:50,000 --> 01:47:03,000
 Then instead of using scale divergence, you have f-GANs and wasp-GANs that use other kinds of divergences and then there is a better version of GANs for text, which is for text scale.

810
01:47:03,000 --> 01:47:09,000
 So generative, adversarial, imitation learning and so on and so forth.

811
01:47:09,000 --> 01:47:17,000
 So but what is the summary? So the good part in green is that likely put free model based on full sample test.

812
01:47:17,000 --> 01:47:29,000
 It uses different forms of divergence and until 2021, GANs gave you the state-of-the-art image generation model and it is able to perform extremely fast sampling.

813
01:47:29,000 --> 01:47:36,000
 Optimization is challenging and you cannot estimate the density because there is no notion of light.

814
01:47:36,000 --> 01:47:46,000
 So other generative approaches, I don't think we have time, so I will just start upon and just mention that there is something called normalizing flows.

815
01:47:46,000 --> 01:47:52,000
 In some sense, this is actually very, very related to VAEs.

816
01:47:52,000 --> 01:47:59,000
 So in VAEs, you just had, you remember the reparameterization trick, you did that trick once.

817
01:47:59,000 --> 01:48:05,000
 If you repeat that trick, that transformation trick many, many times, you get a normalization flow.

818
01:48:05,000 --> 01:48:08,000
 Not normalization flow, normalizing flows.

819
01:48:08,000 --> 01:48:14,000
 And some models that are based on normalizing flows are nice, real MVP flow, etc.

820
01:48:14,000 --> 01:48:24,000
 And diffusion models, it's a little more complicated, but conceptually it's related to normalizing flows in the following way.

821
01:48:24,000 --> 01:48:34,000
 Remember that when we did the reparameterization trick in the VAE, that was a deterministic transformation.

822
01:48:34,000 --> 01:48:40,000
 I did mu or rather z equal to mu plus sigma e.

823
01:48:40,000 --> 01:48:51,000
 Now here, when I convert the x from the z, I do a stochastic transformation.

824
01:48:51,000 --> 01:48:56,000
 So instead of a deterministic transformation, every transformation is a stochastic transformation.

825
01:48:56,000 --> 01:49:01,000
 And this is the essential difference between a diffusion model and a normalizing flow model.

826
01:49:01,000 --> 01:49:12,000
 And the reason you need to know diffusion models, because the latest Dali version, stable diffusion vision, all of these are latent diffusion models.

827
01:49:12,000 --> 01:49:22,000
 Okay, so I will not get into energy based models, but I will just tell you that these are based on undirected graphical models rather than directed graphical model.

828
01:49:22,000 --> 01:49:28,000
 And last but not the least, evaluation of generating models, right.

829
01:49:28,000 --> 01:49:35,000
 I have already mentioned to you that this is extremely hard, and there are multiple aspects that are required for evaluation.

830
01:49:35,000 --> 01:49:42,000
 One is quality. What does quality mean? Are the samples likely under the data distribution?

831
01:49:42,000 --> 01:49:49,000
 What is diversity? Do the samples cover the data distribution there, right?

832
01:49:49,000 --> 01:49:57,000
 So you can see the tradeoff between this. If I am able to generate one perfect image of a cat, is this a good generative model?

833
01:49:57,000 --> 01:50:04,000
 One image is of a perfect cat, but you can generate only one kind of cat. So it is important to have diversity.

834
01:50:04,000 --> 01:50:10,000
 So you have to have the ability to generate different types of cat images, right?

835
01:50:10,000 --> 01:50:21,000
 And then there is generalization, right? Are you only able to generate cat images that are very, very similar to the cats in your database, right?

836
01:50:21,000 --> 01:50:32,000
 So this is called generalization. But here is the tricky thing, right? If you generalize way too much, then your human critique is going to tell you that you are hallucinating.

837
01:50:32,000 --> 01:50:43,000
 Poor LLM, right? Anyway, and then the point is that it is hard to define metrics that cover all the different aspects.

838
01:50:43,000 --> 01:50:52,000
 If you have to do that really, you need something called post facto human evaluation, but this is expensive, slow, unreliable, right?

839
01:50:52,000 --> 01:51:00,000
 So here is the summary. So generative AI are models that mimic the data distribution and enable efficient sampling.

840
01:51:00,000 --> 01:51:04,000
 These are firmly grounded with the concepts of probability.

841
01:51:04,000 --> 01:51:12,000
 The tasks that you need to do with generative AI models are sampling, scoring, learning and inferring latent representation.

842
01:51:12,000 --> 01:51:20,000
 Most important point to remember, not all generative models are good at all of these tasks.

843
01:51:20,000 --> 01:51:33,000
 So depending on your task, you would have to choose a particular kind of generative model that is important for you, depending on which one of the sampling, scoring, learning and inferring is important for your task.

844
01:51:33,000 --> 01:51:43,000
 And finally, as I said before, there's enormous potential for good, but we have to be very, very careful about adverse consequences, okay?

